Usage: agent-deck watcher <command> [args]

Watchers listen for inbound events (webhooks, push notifications,
GitHub events, Slack messages) and route them to conductor sessions
so your running agents can act on them automatically.

Commands:
  create <type> --name <name>   Create a new watcher (types: webhook, ntfy, github, slack)
  start <name>                  Mark a watcher as running (picked up by TUI engine)
  stop <name>                   Mark a watcher as stopped
  list [--json]                 List all watchers with status and event rate
  status <name> [--json]        Show detailed watcher info including recent events
  test <name>                   Route a synthetic event to verify routing config
  routes [--json]               Show all routing rules from clients.json
  import <path>                 Migrate bash issue-watcher channels.json to Go watcher config
  install-skill <skill-name>    Install a skill to ~/.config/agent-deck/skills/pool (e.g. watcher-creator)

Examples (one per adapter type):

  # Generic HTTP webhook listening on port 9000
  agent-deck watcher create webhook --name my-webhook --port 9000

  # GitHub repository webhook with HMAC-SHA256 verification
  # Secret comes from the environment or a chmod-600 file, never a CLI arg:
  GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=... agent-deck watcher create github --name gh-alerts
  agent-deck watcher create github --name gh-alerts --secret-file ~/.gh-webhook-secret

  # ntfy.sh push-notification topic
  agent-deck watcher create ntfy --name phone-alerts --topic my-private-topic

  # Slack-bridged via ntfy topic (see skill for Cloudflare Worker setup)
  agent-deck watcher create slack --name team-slack --topic my-slack-topic

After creating, activate and inspect:
  agent-deck watcher start <name>
  agent-deck watcher list              # see health + events/hour
  agent-deck watcher test <name>       # fire a synthetic event

Routing: edit ~/.local/share/agent-deck/watcher/clients.json to pick which
conductor / group receives events. See `agent-deck watcher routes` for
the currently-loaded rules across all watchers.

Conversational setup (recommended for first-time use):
  agent-deck watcher install-skill watcher-creator
  # Then inside Claude Code in an agent-deck session, ask:
  # "Use the watcher-creator skill to set up a GitHub watcher"

Full reference: https://github.com/asheshgoplani/agent-deck#watchers
